    "created_at": "2023-08-01T03:58:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-08-01T04:00:22Z",
    "body": "I think exposing both the dirty value and the dirty checkedness value is a good idea overall. It should help programmatically determine whether a control's value has been modified or not. Take for example a table of editable data. The user selects rows to edit and enters the editing mode. In this mode, table cells render controls of appropriate types. When the user is done, they click _Save_ and the application handles the submission. Now, you wouldn't want to include all values in the payload, only those that were changed, so you map through each selected row and filter out controls with `value !== defaultValue`. Having the dirtiness flag available would make it more idiomatic and less error-prone.\r\n\r\nTo be honest, I don't really have any compelling argument for this change other than \"it'd be a nice thing to have\". Perhaps there are arguments against it, @domenic?",
